// Review Z6, open question 1: two perform nodes of `run` on the two branches of an `if`
// in a loop. The push of `v` comes first in each turn, so each kept value is made after
// the last push. The facts of the clause join the parameter over the two nodes
// (issue #79): at the second node the value of the first node (made in the turn before,
// before the push) is a value of the parameter. Clean: the program prints 5.
effect Run { fn run(g: fn()) }
fn main() {
    let v = Vec.new()
    v.push(1)
    let keep = || print(0)
    let i = 0
    handle Run { fn run(g) { keep = g
        resume(()) } } in {
        while i < 2 {
            v.push(2)
            if i == 0 { run(|| print(v[0])) } else { run(|| print(5)) }
            i = i + 1
        }
    }
    keep()
}
